{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of volatility: The model consistently forecasts a smooth, monotonic trend (either flat or slowly rising) while true outcomes exhibit sharp spikes (e.g., 70 to 76) followed by rapid mean reversion. Apply a volatility multiplier to forecast ranges to capture these swing events.",
    "Implement a 'Spike-and-Revert' heuristic: When the true price exceeds the model's forecast by more than 5-6 EUR (indicating a spike), immediately adjust subsequent 5-10 day forecasts downward to anticipate the inevitable drop, as the model fails to predict the reversal.",
    "Reduce reliance on sentiment signals during high-volatility regimes: High sentiment scores (1.00) often coincide with the model's failure to predict the magnitude of upward moves or the speed of the subsequent correction. Do not use sentiment to justify the model's smooth trend assumptions.",
    "Apply a lag correction for mean reversion: The model reacts too slowly to price drops after peaks. If the true price falls below the forecast by >3 EUR, reduce the next 3-day forecast by an additional 1-2 EUR to account for the momentum of the decline.",
    "Horizon-specific bias adjustment: The model exhibits a persistent positive bias (overestimation) in the 15-30 day horizon following a price spike. Apply a linear decay correction factor to long-term forecasts in these periods."
  ]
}
